**Read:** [Calibration of temperature and humidity enclosures — EURAMET cg-20](../../../inquiry/metrology/sources/Thermal-Enclosures.lingua#r_4A4JXBQPJ1SXY82FVV367HSN70), the complete enclosure-calibration guide; [Radiance temperature calibrations — NIST SP 250-43](../sources/Radiance-Temperature-Calibrations-NIST.lingua#r_1ZZFTRWTTE4RBKGANZJMEYQ0WE), the relevant full source/detector method; [Introduction to Engineering Thermodynamics — Claire Yu Yan](../sources/Introduction-to-Engineering-Thermodynamics-Yan.lingua#r_6AX93SRMGE0XXJTQ1NW6X9QXTW), temperature and system energy.

After the shared temperature-metrology study, connect the instrument to the thermal model. A probe exchanges energy through contact, stem conduction, convection, radiation and electrical self-heating. Its indicated temperature follows its own energy balance, which may differ from the undisturbed material temperature.

For a lumped sensor with heat capacity C and conductance G to a fixed environment, C dT/dt = G(T∞−T) gives a time constant C/G. This model requires sufficiently small internal gradients and approximately constant coefficients. A measured delay may also include readout filtering or contact changes; fitting one exponential does not prove that only one physical time scale exists.

Specify location, immersion/contact, loading, equilibrium criterion, time response and uncertainty. Compare contact and radiation methods on the same stated measurand. Read the full enclosure and instrument methods, and separate a simulated response study from a performed calibration.

**Continue:** [Energy, power, capacity, and a production denominator](../../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/practice/Energy-power-and-capacity.lingua#r_0EE7P9TMJK8SRVCVM1M7K61RAR).

